---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Clearance.astro';
import Closer from '../../components/clearance/Closer.astro';
import { u } from '../../lib/url';
const c = (p = '') => u(`clearance/${p}`);
const mail = (plan: string) => `mailto:james@hangarplatform.dev?subject=${encodeURIComponent(`Clearance ${plan}`)}`;
---
<Layout title="Pricing" page="pricing" description="Clearance pricing: open enforcement for free, paid plans for hosted, signed, anchored assurance. Metered on governed production scope, never per seat.">
  <section class="page-hero" style="text-align:center">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Pricing</p>
      <h1 class="display" style="margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto">Open enforcement. <em>Paid</em> assurance.</h1>
      <p class="sub center">The controller, the provenance detector, the classifier, the record schema and the verifier are open source. You pay for keeping the assurance current: hosted, witnessed, retained, mapped to your controls, and delivered to the people who have to believe it. Audit firms pay nothing.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="clr-wrap">
    <div class="plans" data-reveal>
      <div class="plan">
        <h3>Community</h3>
        <p class="for">Platform teams evaluating, and home labs.</p>
        <p class="price">Free</p>
        <p class="per">Apache 2.0</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Open controller, binding plugin and admission policy</li>
          <li>Provenance detector and classifier</li>
          <li>Local recorder, 30-day retention</li>
          <li><code>clearance verify</code>: you keep your evidence and can prove it to anyone, by hand</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={c('design-partners/')}>Get early access</a>
      </div>
      <div class="plan pop">
        <span class="badge">Most teams</span>
        <h3>Team</h3>
        <p class="for">One compliance scope.</p>
        <p class="price">$1,500<small> to $2,500 / mo</small></p>
        <p class="per">per governed scope</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Hosted recorder, 1-year retention</li>
          <li>Witnesses you don't run</li>
          <li>SOC 2 change-management packet</li>
          <li>Chat approvals</li>
          <li>Ledger anchoring at included volume</li>
          <li>Everything in Community</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-solid" href={mail('Team')}>Talk to us <span class="arr">→</span></a>
      </div>
      <div class="plan">
        <h3>Business</h3>
        <p class="for">Several scopes or frameworks.</p>
        <p class="price">$5,000<small> to $8,000 / mo</small></p>
        <p class="per">multi-scope</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Retention configurable to 7 years</li>
          <li>PCI DSS and ISO 27001 mappings</li>
          <li>Tower console plugin</li>
          <li>Attested exports, metered anchoring</li>
          <li>Assurance providers: automatic submission per engagement, acceptance receipts in the ledger</li>
          <li>A trust page built from receipts, for vendor-risk reviews</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={mail('Business')}>Talk to us</a>
      </div>
      <div class="plan">
        <h3>Regulated</h3>
        <p class="for">Banks, insurers, public sector.</p>
        <p class="price">From $100k<small> / yr</small></p>
        <p class="per">annual</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Self-hosted GitHub App</li>
          <li>Air-gapped recorder, offline anchoring</li>
          <li>Custom control mappings</li>
          <li>Direct audit-firm integrations</li>
          <li>Data residency, named support</li>
        </ul>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={mail('Regulated')}>Talk to us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="plan wide" data-reveal>
      <div>
        <span class="badge still">For the other side of the table</span>
        <h3>Auditor</h3>
        <p class="for">Audit firms and readiness assessors.</p>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Verification tool</li>
        <li>Witness operation</li>
        <li>Packet intake, in your format</li>
        <li>One-click acceptance that signs a receipt into the ledger</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="wide-cta">
        <p class="price">Free</p>
        <p class="per">always</p>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href={mail('Auditor')}>Talk to us</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note center" style="text-align:center;margin-top:1.4rem">Indicative design-partner pricing in USD: hypotheses to test with the first customers, not benchmarks. Verification is always free, and so is everything on the auditor's side.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="stats" data-reveal>
        <div class="stat"><b>$0</b><span>to verify a packet, for anyone, forever</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>0</b><span>seats to count: agents don't have seats</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>&lt; 1 s</b><span>added to routine tier 0 and 1 changes</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1 hr</b><span>target from Helm install to first cleared change</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="cols" data-reveal>
        <div>
          <p class="kicker">The meter</p>
          <h2 class="h2" style="font-size:clamp(1.8rem,2.6vw + 0.6rem,2.6rem)">Priced on governed production <em>scope</em>. Never per seat.</h2>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p>Per-developer pricing punishes adoption, and agents don't have seats anyway. Clearance meters the production scope it governs, and the contract defines "environment" once, with you, so a confusing meter never becomes a procurement objection.</p>
          <p>What you're paying for: less time reconciling evidence, finding production changes nobody can trace, one enforcement model across repositories and clusters, evidence that flows into the GRC and SIEM tools you already use, and routine changes that keep moving fast.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="clr-wrap">
      <div class="sec-head center" data-reveal>
        <p class="kicker">Questions</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Before you <em>file</em> the flight plan.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq" data-reveal>
        <details open><summary>Will Clearance get us through our audit?</summary><p>Clearance helps you enforce your own change-control policy and assemble evidence for the controls that apply to you. Which evidence satisfies those controls is up to your policy, your scope and your auditor. It never promises an audit outcome, and it never calls its output a certification.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does a standard require an AI approval gate?</summary><p>No, and Clearance won't tell you one does. SOC 2 CC8.1, PCI DSS 6.5.1 and ISO 27001 A.8.32 set objectives: production changes are authorized, tested and traceable. Agents make that harder to show, which is the problem Clearance solves.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What if Clearance goes down on a Friday afternoon?</summary><p>You decide per tier. The defaults let routine tier 0 and 1 changes proceed and hold tier 2 and 3, so an outage never blocks a routine deploy and always holds a secret rotation. Every degraded-mode change is recorded and shows up in the packet's exceptions.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Do you need our prompts and source code?</summary><p>No. Intent is redacted by default to a task reference and a secret-scrubbed summary. Records stay in your tenant. Anything shared across customers is opt-in and aggregate only.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Which platforms are supported?</summary><p>The first release covers GitHub, Argo CD and Kubernetes, with Kyverno or OPA for policy and any OIDC identity provider. Flux and GitLab come when a design partner needs them. Paths Clearance can't see are reported as coverage unavailable, never skipped.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why is the auditor side free?</summary><p>Because the product is the position between you and the people who have to believe your evidence, and that position only exists if the people on the other side use it. An audit firm that has verified one witnessed packet and signed one receipt tends to ask the next client why theirs isn't. Charging them for the tool would be charging the ledger for its own witnesses.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Are these prices final?</summary><p>No. They're hypotheses, set as bands and tested with design partners. The meter stays: governed production scope, never seats. Retention, self-hosting and direct firm integrations cost real engineering time, so they're priced as bands and add-ons rather than folded into a flat fee.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why is the controller open source?</summary><p>A gate a security team can't read is a gate they won't install, and open verification is what makes the ledger a ledger. The paid plans are the hosted, retained, anchored, mapped and supported parts.</p></details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Closer title="Fly a <em>six-week</em> pilot." />
</Layout>
